1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,370
Everything starts at the battery plug, B1, on the underside.

2
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:12,078
The Cyton runs from a battery of 3 to 6 volts only, never from the mains.

3
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:18,270
That's a safety choice: anything connected to your head should be isolated from the wall socket.

4
00:00:19,467 --> 00:00:23,063
The first part after the battery is D9, a diode.

5
00:00:23,063 --> 00:00:25,984
A diode is a one-way valve for current.

6
00:00:25,984 --> 00:00:31,753
If you plug the battery in backwards, D9 blocks it, and nothing gets damaged.

7
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:35,124
The price is a small drop of about 0.3 volts.

8
00:00:35,124 --> 00:00:37,297
What comes out is called RAW.

9
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:41,303
RAW goes to U5, a 3.3 volt regulator.

10
00:00:41,303 --> 00:00:49,429
A regulator takes a voltage that wanders, as the battery drains, and turns it into a perfectly steady one.

11
00:00:49,429 --> 00:01:00,775
Its output, DVDD, powers all the digital parts: the microcontroller, the radio, the accelerometer, the SD card, and the digital side of the ADS1299.

12
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:06,371
A nice detail: the power switch doesn't carry the board's current at all.

13
00:01:06,371 --> 00:01:13,577
It only drives U5's enable pin, which switches the regulator, and the whole board, on and off.

14
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:19,777
The ADS1299's analog side needs two more supplies.

15
00:01:19,777 --> 00:01:23,460
U4 makes plus 2.5 volts, called AVDD.

16
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:30,030
It's fed from the already clean 3.3 volts, so AVDD is extra quiet.

17
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:38,260
The analog side also needs minus 2.5 volts, and a battery can't give you a negative voltage directly.

18
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:39,379
That's U3's job.

19
00:01:39,379 --> 00:01:50,144
It's a charge pump: thousands of times a second, it charges a small capacitor from 3.3 volts, flips it upside down, and pours that charge into its output.

20
00:01:50,144 --> 00:01:52,660
The result is about minus 3.3 volts.

21
00:01:53,867 --> 00:02:03,479
That flipped voltage is a bit rippled, so VR1, a negative regulator, cleans it into a steady minus 2.5 volts, called AVSS.

22
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:07,025
Now the ADS1299 has plus and minus 2.5 volts:

23
00:02:07,025 --> 00:02:09,704
5 volts in total, centred on zero.

24
00:02:09,704 --> 00:02:19,317
Centring on zero means the brain signal, which swings both ways around zero, sits right in the middle of the chip's range.

25
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:23,781
Next to every regulator you'll see small capacitors.

26
00:02:23,781 --> 00:02:32,338
Think of them as little water tanks: when a chip suddenly gulps current, the capacitor supplies it instantly, so the voltage doesn't dip.

27
00:02:32,338 --> 00:02:34,899
Regulators also need them to stay stable.

28
00:02:34,899 --> 00:02:40,083
In the schematic, this whole chain sits in the power supply block, at the top left.

29
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:43,430
Why so many separate supplies?

30
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:45,737
Because digital chips are noisy.

31
00:02:45,737 --> 00:02:55,038
Every time the microcontroller or the radio switches, it pulls quick spikes of current, and those spikes ripple along its supply.

32
00:02:55,038 --> 00:03:03,402
If the ADS1299's analog side shared that supply, the ripple would land right on top of your microvolt brain signals.

33
00:03:03,402 --> 00:03:13,641
So the analog side gets its own, extra-clean rails, AVDD and AVSS, made by their own low-noise regulators, while the digital parts share DVDD.

34
00:03:13,641 --> 00:03:18,977
Keeping analog and digital apart is one of the golden rules of EEG design.

35
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,455
Chapter recap.

36
00:03:21,455 --> 00:03:24,684
Battery, then D9, the one-way valve.

37
00:03:24,684 --> 00:03:28,898
Then U5 makes 3.3 volts for everything digital.

38
00:03:28,898 --> 00:03:37,776
U4 makes plus 2.5 volts, and U3 with VR1 makes minus 2.5 volts, for the analog side of the ADS1299.

39
00:03:37,776 --> 00:03:42,170
And capacitors everywhere, as little water tanks.
